## What this rule is for

Use this for any non-code writing: chat replies, docs, issues, PR descriptions, Notion notes.
If this rule conflicts with a task requirement, follow the task requirement.

## Default voice

- Write like a human. Skip marketing fluff and corporate jargon.
- Be direct. Don’t soften with “I think,” “maybe,” or “could.”
- Prefer active voice.
- Prefer “you” over “we” when giving instructions.
- Use contractions (“you’ll,” “won’t,” “can’t”) when it reads naturally.

## Make it concrete

- Replace vague claims with specifics (examples, numbers, edge cases, file names).
- Prefer short bullets. Use numbered steps only when sequence matters.

## Avoid common AI tells

- Don’t start with canned intros (“Great question,” “Let me help you,” “Let’s dive into…”).
- Don’t add essay closers (“In conclusion,” “Overall,” “To summarize…”).
- Don’t add self-referential disclaimers (“As an AI…”).
- Avoid em dashes; use commas, semicolons, or separate sentences.

## Words to avoid (quick list)

- Avoid these fillers: “very,” “really,” “pretty,” “quite,” “arguably,” “innovative,” “robust,” “seamless.”
- Prefer these swaps:
  - “implement” → “do” / “build”
  - “utilize” → “use”
  - “assistance” → “help”
  - “commence” → “start”
  - “best practices” → “proven approaches”

## Titles

- Make the title a promise (“How to ___ without ___”).
- Avoid “My thoughts on ___.”